  year = {2026},
  note = {Fermionic matter sub-programme companion note, logically between the Schur reduction (PRS) and the physical identification of the chiral generator. For every smooth self-adjoint projector family $1-P(s) = Q_0 + sQ_1 + s^2Q_2 + O(s^3)$ the projector constraints force $Q_1 = [A, Q_0]$ to be a purely off-diagonal Grassmann tangent and pin the diagonal blocks of $Q_2$ to $Q_1^2$ with opposite signs; the jet parities alternate under $J_\Pi$-equivariance. With a covariant moving spinorial embedding and a $J_\Pi$-even Dirac operator the first-order $J_\Pi$-odd part of $E_\Pi^2$ is $\{E_0, E_1\}$, and antiunitary parity makes every odd-order generation-mixing coefficient vanish, mixing at even orders not being excluded. An explicit covariant moving-Schur family realises $E_0^2 = \mathrm{diag}(1, \tfrac12, \tfrac12)$ with a non-zero split rate $u'(0) = 11 \cdot 2^{1/4}/9$. In the chiral frame $[\Pi_{J_\Pi\text{-odd}}\dot Q(0)]_{LL} = \tfrac12\partial_s\Delta_\chi(P)|_0$ determines one block of the odd tangent, not a unique generator; the selection of the family, the localisation, and the normalisation of $|u|$ remain open,
